1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,278
Hi! Travel stories are always exciting.

2
00:00:03,278 --> 00:00:09,330
Today we'll learn to talk about places you've visited with '가 본 적이 있어요',

3
00:00:09,330 --> 00:00:12,020
and future plans with '할 계획이에요' [hal gye-hoe-gi-e-yo].

4
00:00:16,510 --> 00:00:21,829
Just two things: talking about your past '경험' [gyeong-heom] (experience),

5
00:00:21,829 --> 00:00:24,444
and your future '계획' [gye-hoek] (plans).

6
00:00:24,444 --> 00:00:28,050
Let's connect past and future naturally.

7
00:00:32,870 --> 00:00:34,829
Some places out there you've been to,

8
00:00:34,829 --> 00:00:36,523
others you want to visit, right?

9
00:00:36,523 --> 00:00:38,270
Let's say that feeling in Korean.

10
00:00:42,030 --> 00:00:47,409
'여행' [yeo-haeng] means travel — going somewhere and coming back.

11
00:00:47,409 --> 00:00:50,823
Use it like '여행을 가다' [yeo-haeng-eul ga-da] or '여행을 하다' [yeo-haeng-eul ha-da].

12
00:00:50,823 --> 00:00:54,340
In summer I travel with my family.

13
00:00:55,320 --> 00:01:00,154
'경험' [gyeong-heom] means experience — something you've done.

14
00:01:00,154 --> 00:01:02,991
Travel '경험' [gyeong-heom] stays with you.

15
00:01:02,991 --> 00:01:07,510
Say '좋은 경험이었어요' [jo-eun gyeong-heo-mi-eo-sseo-yo] — it was a good experience.

16
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:14,912
'명소' [myeong-so] means a famous spot many people visit.

17
00:01:14,912 --> 00:01:17,665
Every city has great '명소' [myeong-so].

18
00:01:17,665 --> 00:01:21,900
That one out there is a well-known '명소' [myeong-so].

19
00:01:27,470 --> 00:01:32,390
'예약' [ye-yak] means reservation — booking in advance.

20
00:01:32,390 --> 00:01:37,087
Before a trip, you '예약' [ye-yak] tickets and rooms.

21
00:01:37,087 --> 00:01:40,330
Ask '예약했어요? ' [ye-ya-kae-sseo-yo] — did you book?

22
00:01:43,230 --> 00:01:48,356
'숙소' [suk-sso] means accommodation — where you sleep on a trip.

23
00:01:48,356 --> 00:01:51,838
Hotels and guesthouses are all '숙소' [suk-sso].

24
00:01:51,838 --> 00:01:55,610
'숙소를 예약했어요' [suk-sso-reul ye-ya-kae-sseo-yo] — I booked a place to stay.

25
00:01:57,580 --> 00:02:01,684
'계획' [gye-hoek] means plan — what you've decided to do.

26
00:02:01,684 --> 00:02:04,482
Use '~할 계획이에요' [hal gye-hoe-gi-e-yo] for the future.

27
00:02:04,482 --> 00:02:07,560
I plan to go hiking this weekend.

28
00:02:10,510 --> 00:02:12,210
Now, experiences.

29
00:02:12,210 --> 00:02:18,010
Add '에 가 본 적이 있어요' after a place to say you've been there.

30
00:02:18,010 --> 00:02:20,810
Repeat slowly: '가 본 적이 있어요'.

31
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:31,554
For example: '제주도에 가 본 적이 있어요' — I've been to Jeju Island,

32
00:02:31,554 --> 00:02:34,133
a place with beautiful blue seas.

33
00:02:34,133 --> 00:02:36,400
Point far away as you say it.

34
00:02:37,620 --> 00:02:42,003
One more: '서울에 가 본 적이 있어요' — I've been to Seoul.

35
00:02:42,003 --> 00:02:46,020
Where have you been? Think of it and say it.

36
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:53,254
Add how many times: '두 번 가 본 적이 있어요' — I've been twice.

37
00:02:53,254 --> 00:02:56,790
If many times: '여러 번 가 본 적이 있어요'.

38
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:05,893
Use it for any experience: '한 적이 있어요' [han jeo-gi i-sseo-yo] means you have the experience; if not,

39
00:03:05,893 --> 00:03:07,630
answer '한 적이 없어요' [han jeo-gi eop-sseo-yo].

40
00:03:11,230 --> 00:03:13,084
Now the future.

41
00:03:13,084 --> 00:03:21,243
Add '할 계획이에요' [hal gye-hoe-gi-e-yo] after what you intend to do: '갈 계획이에요' [gal gye-hoe-gi-e-yo] (plan to go),

42
00:03:21,243 --> 00:03:24,210
'볼 계획이에요' [bol gye-hoe-gi-e-yo] (plan to see).

43
00:03:25,460 --> 00:03:28,465
For example: '다음엔 부산에 갈 계획이에요' — Next,

44
00:03:28,465 --> 00:03:30,205
I plan to go to Busan,

45
00:03:30,205 --> 00:03:32,974
famous for its sea and night views.

46
00:03:32,974 --> 00:03:35,900
Say it dreamily, watching the sunset.

47
00:03:36,350 --> 00:03:44,080
Not just places — also things to do: '주말에 등산할 계획이에요' [ju-ma-re deung-san-hal gye-hoe-gi-e-yo] — I plan to hike this weekend.

48
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:47,340
'쉴 계획이에요' [swil gye-hoe-gi-e-yo] (plan to rest) works too.

49
00:03:47,790 --> 00:03:53,829
Before a trip you prepare: '숙소를 예약할 계획이에요' [suk-sso-reul ye-ya-kal gye-hoe-gi-e-yo] — I plan to book a place to stay.

50
00:03:53,829 --> 00:03:56,530
Put today's words into your plans.

51
00:03:56,980 --> 00:03:59,519
You can ask, too: '언제 갈 계획이에요?

52
00:03:59,519 --> 00:04:01,804
' — When do you plan to go?

53
00:04:01,804 --> 00:04:04,850
That's how you hear someone's plans.

54
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:10,504
A real conversation. If asked '제주도에 가 본 적이 있어요?

55
00:04:10,504 --> 00:04:13,272
' (Have you been to Jeju?

56
00:04:13,272 --> 00:04:17,148
), answer '네, 두 번 가 본 적이 있어요' (Yes,

57
00:04:17,148 --> 00:04:19,030
I've been twice).

58
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:24,224
Follow experience with a plan: '두 번 가 본 적이 있어요.

59
00:04:24,224 --> 00:04:25,838
다음엔 부산에 갈 계획이에요.

60
00:04:25,838 --> 00:04:29,270
' — Past and future in one breath!

61
00:04:30,390 --> 00:04:31,473
Your turn.

62
00:04:31,473 --> 00:04:38,726
Think of one place you've been and one you'll go: '___에 가 본 적이 있어요.

63
00:04:38,726 --> 00:04:40,350
___에 갈 계획이에요. '

64
00:04:44,540 --> 00:04:52,416
Recap. Words: 여행·경험·계획·명소·예약·숙소.

65
00:04:52,416 --> 00:05:03,000
Experience: '가 본 적이 있어요'. Plans: '할 계획이에요' [hal gye-hoe-gi-e-yo].

66
00:05:05,610 --> 00:05:06,276
Great job!

67
00:05:06,276 --> 00:05:09,741
Make your own travel story with today's expressions.

68
00:05:09,741 --> 00:05:10,940
See you next time!

